    "difficulty": "Mastery",
    "difficultyReason": "Generalise the scaffolded continue-then-stop pattern and connect the infinite collection of stopping outcomes to total probability, accounting for zero probability of continuing forever.",
    "assessment": {
      "model": "gpt-6.1-sol",
      "assessedAt": "2026-10-21T04:54:53.973Z",
      "rubricVersion": "mathsmap-cognitive-demand-v1",
      "taskHash": "93feb1ad4634a643a7fad894f66b059160498effaeb3526f8064dfa213a7902b",
      "evidence": ".booklet-work/reasoning-ratings/calls/enrichment-maths-2-part11-20261009/644e45a5a968862bfa52c6b0e194492ea691cd6a811c7fd6660d8d04766ba9aa-1792558434878/last-message.txt"
    }
  },
  "content": {
    "id": "p26-q32-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "32",
    "prompt": "Let $0<p<1$. Sam is about to start running laps on the track. At the beginning of each lap, he will run another lap with probability $p$, and decide to stop for the day with probability $1-p$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p26-q32-a",
        "type": "group",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the probability that Sam will run:",
        "layout": "grid",
        "columns": 3,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [
          {
            "id": "p26-q32-a-i",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "i",
            "prompt": "0 laps",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$1-p$",
              "worked": "Sam stops before running any laps, with probability $1-p$.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 12
          },
          {
            "id": "p26-q32-a-ii",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "ii",
            "prompt": "1 lap",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$(1-p)p$",
              "worked": "Sam runs one lap and then stops.\n$$\\Pr(\\text{1 lap})=p(1-p).$$",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 16
          },
          {
            "id": "p26-q32-a-iii",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "iii",
            "prompt": "2 laps",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$(1-p)p^2$",
              "worked": "Sam runs two laps and then stops.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(\\text{2 laps})&=p\\times p\\times(1-p)\\\\\\Pr(\\text{2 laps})&=p^2(1-p).\\end{align*}$$",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 20
          },
          {
            "id": "p26-q32-a-iv",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "iv",
            "prompt": "3 laps",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$(1-p)p^3$",
              "worked": "Sam runs three laps and then stops.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(\\text{3 laps})&=p^3\\times(1-p)\\\\\\Pr(\\text{3 laps})&=p^3(1-p).\\end{align*}$$",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 20
          },
          {
            "id": "p26-q32-a-v",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "v",
            "prompt": "4 laps",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$(1-p)p^4$",
              "worked": "Sam runs four laps and then stops.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(\\text{4 laps})&=p^4\\times(1-p)\\\\\\Pr(\\text{4 laps})&=p^4(1-p).\\end{align*}$$",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 20
          },
          {
            "id": "p26-q32-a-vi",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "vi",
            "prompt": "$n$ laps",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$(1-p)p^n$",
              "worked": "Sam continues $n$ times, each with probability $p$, and then stops with probability $1-p$.\n$$\\Pr(n\\text{ laps})=p^n(1-p).$$",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 24
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q32-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Deduce that $1=(1-p)(1+p+p^2+p^3+\\cdots)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1=(1-p)(1+p+p^2+p^3+\\cdots)$.\n\nSam eventually stops with probability 1, so the probabilities of all possible lap counts sum to 1.",
          "worked": "Since $0<p<1$, the probability of continuing forever is 0. Sam therefore eventually stops with probability 1. The outcomes of running 0, 1, 2, … laps are mutually exclusive and exhaustive, so\n$$\\begin{align*}1&=(1-p)+(1-p)p+(1-p)p^2+(1-p)p^3+\\cdots\\\\1&=(1-p)(1+p+p^2+p^3+\\cdots).\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      }
    ]
  },
